WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:04.160
Johnny Knoxville is one of America's&nbsp;
most recognizable daredevils, but which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:08.880
stunt forced him to use a catheter for three&nbsp;
years? You might not want to know. The answer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:12.880
and more things you may not know about&nbsp;
the "Jackass Forever" star, just ahead.

00:00:12.880 --> 00:00:17.440
Johnny Knoxville — born Philip John Clapp — is&nbsp;
no stranger to injuries and hospital visits.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.720
But he also, unfortunately, had&nbsp;
many medical issues as a child.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:24.640
In an interview with Rolling Stone, he&nbsp;
details the asthma issues he had as a kid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:27.520
and which he still deals with&nbsp;
today. He told the magazine,

00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:31.680
"It was a really nice childhood, and I was&nbsp;
really coddled. But I was also sick a lot."

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:35.120
His asthma was so bad that he couldn't&nbsp;
do a lot of classic childhood activities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.120 --> 00:00:39.440
like playing in a pile of leaves. Knoxville even&nbsp;
had stints in which he had to spend several days&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.920
to a week in the hospital. He said he almost died&nbsp;
at the age of eight as a result of having the flu,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:48.720
pneumonia, and bronchitis all at the same time.&nbsp;
Yet his chronic lung disease didn't stop him from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.720
playing sports because he, quote, "didn't&nbsp;
want to be left out or feel like a victim."

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:56.640
Johnny Knoxville is technically a classically&nbsp;
trained actor ... even though his training was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.640 --> 00:01:01.200
never formally completed. According to the New&nbsp;
York Times, Knoxville moved to Los Angeles after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.200 --> 00:01:05.840
high school and attended the Pasadena School of&nbsp;
Dramatic Arts, but he dropped out after six weeks.

00:01:05.840 --> 00:01:09.120
Knoxville told the Washington Times that&nbsp;
he had wanted to be an actor since he was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:13.440
13, quote, "because it sounded like a job with&nbsp;
the least amount of work involved." As luck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.440 --> 00:01:17.360
would have it, Knoxville would eventually make&nbsp;
it in the acting world without a fancy degree.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:21.040
Yet it took some time and a unique career&nbsp;
path before he made his way to stardom.

00:01:21.040 --> 00:01:25.120
Before "Jackass" was born, Knoxville was a&nbsp;
writer for skateboarding magazine Big Brother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:27.760
where he pitched a story about&nbsp;
testing self-defense equipment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:31.840
like pepper spray and stun guns, on&nbsp;
himself. Magazine editor Jeff Tremaine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:35.680
suggested Knoxville film the experiments&nbsp;
and the rest, as they say, is history.

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:41.040
Knoxville's inaugural acting credit was an&nbsp;
appearance on "The Ben Stiller Show" in 1992.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.960
It wasn't a major role, as Knoxville&nbsp;
was a featured extra as a Cure fan.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.160
Other roles audiences might have missed&nbsp;
Knoxville in include a "college guy"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.160 --> 00:01:53.360
in "Coyote Ugly" in 2002, as well&nbsp;
as Bob in "Desert Blues" in 1995.

00:01:53.360 --> 00:01:56.160
Though he had already made a name for&nbsp;
himself by the time he took the part,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.160 --> 00:02:00.560
audiences might be surprised to learn that he&nbsp;
voiced Johnny Krill on "SpongeBob SquarePants."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.560 --> 00:02:04.160
But while Knoxville has played a plethora&nbsp;
of roles outside of the "Jackass" franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.160 --> 00:02:08.320
most people would agree that his greatest&nbsp;
performances happen when he's just being himself.

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.240
Knoxville is widely known as a stunt&nbsp;
performer and actor. But fans might not know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:16.880
that he also has numerous writing credits.&nbsp;
Knoxville has 15 writing credits on IMDB,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:21.200
including projects such as "Jackass Presents:&nbsp;
Bad Grandpa," "Action Point," and many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:21.200 --> 00:02:26.080
others — including the mainline "Jackass"&nbsp;
movies. He told GQ in a 2021 interview,

00:02:26.080 --> 00:02:29.760
"Writing gave me confidence as a person. It&nbsp;
was like, I don't have to just worry about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:33.280
trying to make it. I can do this&nbsp;
and feel satisfied and engaged."

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:36.080
Luckily for fans and Knoxville&nbsp;
alike, the writer-turned-actor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.440
did make it –– and he even turned down&nbsp;
some big opportunities along the way.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.000
Many actors have become mega-famous&nbsp;
after appearing on NBC's iconic sketch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.440
comedy show "Saturday Night Live."&nbsp;
But Johnny Knoxville turned down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:50.640
long-running show right around when&nbsp;
he was shooting the "Jackass" pilot.

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:55.280
In a 2005 interview with the Washington Times,&nbsp;
Knoxville detailed his decision, saying,

00:02:55.280 --> 00:02:58.880
"It was at the point where I either say yes&nbsp;
to my friends, where we had all the control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.880 --> 00:03:02.080
or yes to 'Saturday Night Live,' where&nbsp;
none of my friends were really going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.080 --> 00:03:05.520
be there and I had no control. I just&nbsp;
thought I made the right decision."

00:03:05.520 --> 00:03:08.160
Knoxville told the Hollywood&nbsp;
Reporter that his role on SNL&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.160 --> 00:03:11.760
would have consisted of him doing solo&nbsp;
stunts each week. He told the outlet,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:12.800
“They were going to give me, like,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.920
five minutes each week to do what&nbsp;
I do, but that would just be me.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:21.200
And I couldn’t do both. Our show was about to go.&nbsp;
So I kind of bet on us as opposed to on myself."

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:24.320
"I wasn't gonna have complete&nbsp;
control like we did on 'Jackass.'"

00:03:24.320 --> 00:03:26.560
"Right. It was a smart move."

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:28.960
In 2005, Knoxville hosted "SNL,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:32.320
proving that — with a little patience —&nbsp;
you can have your cake and eat it, too.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:36.320
Johnny Knoxville might live a wild&nbsp;
and crazy lifestyle on the screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.320 --> 00:03:40.160
but, off-screen, he's a family man. He's&nbsp;
a father of three and has been married&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:43.440
twice. Knoxville and his ex-wife&nbsp;
Melanie Cates had a daughter named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:48.400
Madison — now an adult who lives in Austin&nbsp;
as of 2021. Yet the two have since divorced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:53.440
and Knoxville is now married to Naomi Nelson. The&nbsp;
couple had two children together, Rocco and Arlo.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:56.000
To no one's surprise, Knoxville&nbsp;
doesn't want his kids to follow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.000 --> 00:04:00.000
in his stunt-performing footsteps.&nbsp;
He told Access in a 2010 interview,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:04.240
"Pranks are fine, no stunts. I don't&nbsp;
want Rocco or Madison doing any stunts."

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.120
As you'd expect, his parenting techniques are far&nbsp;
different than his acting methods. As GQ noted in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.840
a 2021 interview, quote "[Knoxville] is notably&nbsp;
attentive to the physical safety of his children."

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.680
Johnny Knoxville has endured his fair&nbsp;
share of injuries over the years while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.680 --> 00:04:21.280
shooting "Jackass," but there's one&nbsp;
incident that really stands out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:25.360
In 2007, Knoxville tore his urethra&nbsp;
during a motorcycle stunt gone wrong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:28.960
while shooting "Jackass Presents: Mat&nbsp;
Hoffman's Tribute to Evel Knievel".

00:04:28.960 --> 00:04:32.080
"I came off the ramp, I let go of&nbsp;
the bike, it goes 20 feet in the air&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:34.640
and comes down and breaks its&nbsp;
handlebars off in my crotch."

00:04:37.360 --> 00:04:40.000
Knoxville said in a 2021 GQ interview,

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:43.280
"I wasn't even supposed to do anything. I&nbsp;
think I just showed up that day and someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.280 --> 00:04:47.600
kind of threw out that I should try and backflip&nbsp;
a motorcycle. I'm like, 'Oh, yeah, I got that.'"

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:51.280
The result? The crash led to Knoxville&nbsp;
having to self-administer a catheter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.280 --> 00:04:55.440
for the next three years. But he&nbsp;
took it all in stride, telling GQ,

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:59.520
“At the time, I was like, 'I can't wait to tell&nbsp;
this story.' Everybody loves a good story."

